1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,105
Good evening from the OROS closing desk.

2
00:00:03,105 --> 00:00:09,005
This morning the system named no pick, so tonight there is nothing to grade.

3
00:00:09,005 --> 00:00:13,740
No setup, no trade, no loss — that is the discipline working.

4
00:00:13,740 --> 00:00:18,676
Why we grade in public: a forecast that is never checked is just content.

5
00:00:18,676 --> 00:00:24,085
Every morning call gets an evening verdict, and both go in the permanent record.

6
00:00:24,050 --> 00:00:27,313
On the day, the book is up 636 dollars.

7
00:00:27,313 --> 00:00:30,157
MetLife (MET) leads, +2.6 percent.

8
00:00:30,157 --> 00:00:33,168
Arista Networks (ANET) lags at -0.2.

9
00:00:33,168 --> 00:00:37,686
The checklist is clear: nothing requires action today.

10
00:00:37,686 --> 00:00:41,212
On screen, the day's gainers and laggards inside the paper book.

11
00:00:41,169 --> 00:00:48,521
And here is the book itself — the actual holdings, identical to this morning's emailed report.

12
00:00:48,521 --> 00:00:57,125
10 positions, led by NVIDIA, ticker NVDA, AbbVie, ticker ABBV, Taiwan Semiconductor Manufacturing, ticker TSM.

13
00:00:57,125 --> 00:01:07,762
Every line carries its share count, its entry, and its exit — because a position without a defined exit is not a position, it is a hope.

14
00:01:07,732 --> 00:01:12,943
Now the part that makes this system different: the loop.

15
00:01:12,943 --> 00:01:15,549
Every morning OROS proposes.

16
00:01:15,549 --> 00:01:26,995
Every evening it grades its own call, journals the verdict, and adjusts its own risk before it is allowed to propose again.

17
00:01:26,995 --> 00:01:31,276
Inside the book today: 8 positions up, 2 down.

18
00:01:31,276 --> 00:01:33,789
Best, TSM at +2.75 percent.

19
00:01:33,789 --> 00:01:35,743
Worst, ASML at -0.40.

20
00:01:35,743 --> 00:01:41,140
Both go in the journal — wins and losses get the same ink.

21
00:01:41,140 --> 00:01:48,957
The honest scoreboard reads: expectancy -3.00% per trade over 1042 graded positions.

22
00:01:48,957 --> 00:01:56,588
Because that record is soft, the system has throttled itself to 50% position size.

23
00:01:56,588 --> 00:02:02,172
Nobody told it to — full size must be earned back with wins.

24
00:02:02,172 --> 00:02:15,572
What tonight's read hands to tomorrow's open: 177 names close the day in Stage 2; position size stays cut to 50% until the record earns it back.

25
00:02:15,534 --> 00:02:17,857
Over to the sports desk.

26
00:02:17,857 --> 00:02:27,440
Day 67 of the race: three books, the same one hundred thousand simulated dollars, since July sixth.

27
00:02:27,440 --> 00:02:33,925
In first place, the live self-rebuilding book, in blue, at 103,455.

28
00:02:33,925 --> 00:02:39,636
Then the index fund, in gray, 0.1 thousand back at 103,391.

29
00:02:39,636 --> 00:02:47,090
Then the frozen first portfolio, the green line, 6.7 thousand back at 96,745.

30
00:02:47,090 --> 00:02:49,800
The race has no finish line.

31
00:02:49,800 --> 00:02:54,253
Same books, same dollars, back at it tomorrow.

32
00:02:54,253 --> 00:02:59,286
Every line starts the same day with the same dollar.

33
00:02:59,286 --> 00:03:01,222
Nothing is restated.

34
00:03:01,222 --> 00:03:10,130
On screen, three equity lines grown from the same starting dollar: the frozen first book, the live book, and the index fund.

35
00:03:10,092 --> 00:03:17,427
All figures are simulated paper trading, graded and journaled by the OROS agents with no human intervention.

36
00:03:17,427 --> 00:03:19,668
Nothing here is financial advice.

37
00:03:19,668 --> 00:03:22,657
From the closing desk — see you at the open.
